"Premature optimization is the root of all evil." — Donald Knuth The 3-step performance workflow: Measure first - Profile to find actual bottlenecks (not guessed ones) Optimize strategically - Focus on hot paths (80/20 rule) Verify improvements - Benchmark before/after to confirm gains Anti-pattern: // ❌ Premature optimization - adds complexity without measurement
pub fn compute_distance(&self, a: &[f32], b: &[f32]) -> f32 { // Complex SIMD intrinsics before profiling shows it's a bottleneck unsafe { use std::arch::x86_64::*; // ... 50 lines of unsafe SIMD code }
} Correct approach: // ✅ Start simple, profile, then optimize if needed
pub fn compute_distance(&self, a: &[f32], b: &[f32]) -> f32 { a.iter() .zip(b.iter()) .map(|(x, y)| (x - y).powi(2)) .sum::<f32>() .sqrt()
}
// Profile shows this is 2% of runtime → don't optimize
// Profile shows this is 60% of runtime → optimize with trueno SIMD

